// Attack.bs -- ticket 59's forged-record scenario, built literally.
//
// `Order` and `Invoice` share an identical field shape (Id: int, Total: int),
// which is ticket 26's whole reason for minting a tag: under structural typing
// alone they would be one type. `Wrapper` is an ORDINARY alias -- not a
// `record` -- so it mints no tag of its own and its `Payload` field is read by
// plain projection, which performs no shape check at all.
//
// `Outer` is EXPORTED. Its own parameter type is `Wrapper`, which is neither a
// closed record (so `record_tag/2` finds no `Kind` field to test) nor an
// int-only type -- so under the standing rule ("only where the function's own
// body would not object") `Outer` gets NO boundary guard at all, and its body
// does not object either: `w.Payload` is one `map_get`, unconditional, and
// whatever comes back is handed on unexamined.
//
// `Inner` is PRIVATE and declares `Order`. Every beam-sharp call site that
// reaches it (there is exactly one, `Outer`'s body) has already been through
// the type checker, which never lets a raw `Wrapper.Payload` projection stand
// for a verified `Order` -- so from beam-sharp's own point of view nothing
// here is unsound. The question is what a caller `bsc`/the type checker never
// saw does to it.
module Attack

record Order   { Id: int, Total: int }
record Invoice { Id: int, Total: int }

type Wrapper = { Payload: Order }

public atom Outer(Wrapper w)

Outer(w) -> Inner(w.Payload)

private atom Inner(Order o)

Inner(o) -> :accepted
